EN["hari-28"] = {
id: "hari-28",
track: "30 Days",
title: "MOCK EXAM 3 (FINAL): 50 Questions / 3 Hours",
sub: "Day 28 \u00b7 \u00b18 hrs \u00b7 Mock Exam 3 (final): 50 questions / 3 hours",
sections: [
{h: "Final briefing: simulating real exam pressure", body: `
<p>This is the LAST mock before the Final Gate (Day 30). Same rules \u2014 <b>50 questions, 3 hours, \u226570% target</b> \u2014 but the level <b>rises</b>: more Hard questions, subtler traps, and several <b>cross-topic</b> questions (e.g. M&A synergies changing NOL utilization; tax shields changing CFO).</p>
<div class="tblwrap"><table class="brutal">
<tr><th>Mock 3 vs Mocks 1\u20132</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
<tr><td>More Hard</td><td>16 Hard questions (vs 10) \u2014 simulating real exam pressure</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cross-topic questions</td><td>Answers needing 2 topics at once (accounting \u00d7 tax; M&A \u00d7 NOLs; LBO \u00d7 dividends)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Subtler traps</td><td>Distractors = "almost right" numbers from common mistakes \u2014 not nonsense answers</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p>Treat this as the <b>dress rehearsal for the Final Gate</b>: same conditions, same timer, same grading standard. If you pass here comfortably, Day 30 is a formality.</p>
<div class="callout"><b>Why make the last mock harder than the pass mark?</b> Because the real exam doesn't care that you "almost passed". Training above the bar = margin of safety for exam day: nerves, unlucky questions, and 3 hours of fatigue. Training target: 80%+; exam target: 70%+.</div>`},
{h: "Exam-day strategy: the 180-minute tactics", body: `
<p>Tactics used by professional exam takers \u2014 for this Mock 3 and the Final Gate:</p>
<ol>
<li><b>0\u201310 min: sweep the Easy ones.</b> Tag each question E/M/H on first read. Do all Easy (\u00b12 min) \u2014 bank certain points before touching the heavy ones.</li>
<li><b>10\u2013120 min: chained Mediums.</b> Multi-step? Write the chain first (EV \u2192 equity \u2192 shares), then compute. One finished chain = verify each bridge before moving on.</li>
<li><b>120\u2013160 min: flagged Hards.</b> Never spend >8 minutes on one question. Still stuck? Pick the most sensible answer, flag it, move on.</li>
<li><b>160\u2013180 min: verify.</b> Return to flagged ones + sanity checks: \u0394WC sign right? TV discounted to the right year? MoIC \u2212 1 = gain?</li>
</ol>
<p><b>Two prohibitions:</b> (1) don't revise an answer without a concrete reason \u2014 first instinct is more often right; (2) don't let one question eat 15 minutes \u2014 15 minutes = 4 lost Easy questions.</p>
<div class="callout"><b>Why Easy first instead of question order?</b> Because Easy and Hard pay the SAME (1 point) but cost 4\u00d7 different time. Maximum score = optimal time allocation, not heroics. An exam is an expectations game, not a heroism game.</div>`},
{h: "\u23F1\uFE0F 3-hour exam-condition timer", body: `
<div class="callout">\u23F1\uFE0F <b>3-hour timer</b> \u2014 click Start, work the 50 questions without breaks: <span id="mocktimer3" style="font-weight:bold;font-size:1.3rem">180:00</span>
<button class="btn small blue" onclick="var s=10800,btn=this;btn.disabled=true;var iv=setInterval(function(){s--;var m=Math.floor(s/60),ss=s%60;var el=document.getElementById('mocktimer3');if(!el){clearInterval(iv);return;}el.textContent=m+':'+(ss<10?'0':'')+ss;if(s<=0){clearInterval(iv);el.textContent='\u26D4 TIME UP \u2014 stop and grade.';}},1000);">\u25B6 Start timer</button>
<p style="font-size:.85rem;margin-top:8px"><b>Honest exam rules:</b> no peeking at notes, no breaks, the full 3 hours. This is the dress rehearsal \u2014 treat it like the real thing.</p></div>`},
{h: "Final debrief + Final Gate readiness checklist", body: `
<p>After grading, do the last debrief with trap classification (calculation / concept / misread). Then check the Final Gate readiness list:</p>
<div class="tblwrap"><table class="brutal">
<tr><th>Checklist</th><th>Standard</th><th>Status</th></tr>
<tr><td>Mock 3 score</td><td>\u226570% (training target 80%+)</td><td>__ /50</td></tr>
<tr><td>Every topic \u226570%</td><td>No topic below the bar</td><td>Yes / No \u2014 name them:</td></tr>
<tr><td>Recurring traps</td><td>No trap type appearing in 2+ mocks</td><td>Yes / No \u2014 name them:</td></tr>
<tr><td>Time management</td><td>Finished without rushing the last 20 minutes</td><td>Yes / No</td></tr>
<tr><td>2 capstones</td><td>Capstone I (Days 12\u201313) + Capstone II (Day 26) approved</td><td>Yes / Not yet</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p>Checklist incomplete \u2192 <b>Day 29</b> is the last repair + personal cheat sheet. Checklist complete \u2192 Day 29 is consolidation, Day 30 the Final Gate.</p>
<div class="callout"><b>Why a checklist instead of "feeling ready"?</b> Because pre-exam feelings always lie \u2014 sometimes overconfident, sometimes panicking. A checklist = written evidence. The Final Gate doesn't grade your feelings; it grades the next 50 questions.</div>`}
],
quiz: [
{q: "Beginning retained earnings Rp15,000k, net income Rp6,500k, dividends Rp4,000k. Ending retained earnings?", opts: ["Rp17,500k","Rp21,500k","Rp25,500k","Rp13,500k"], answer: 0, k: "akuntansi", diff: "Easy",
 hints: ["15,000 + 6,500 \u2212 4,000 = ?","Trace it one by one: IS first, then BS, then CFS."],
 explain: "15,000 + 6,500 \u2212 4,000 = 17,500."},
{q: "The company repays a Rp5,000k bank loan in cash. The trail across the 3 statements?", opts: ["IS: 5,000 expense","IS untouched; BS: cash \u22125,000 & debt \u22125,000; CFS: financing \u22125,000","CFS: operating \u22125,000","BS: equity \u22125,000"], answer: 1, k: "linkage", diff: "Easy",
 hints: ["Repaying debt = returning principal, not an expense.","Cash to lenders = financing activity."],
 explain: "IS untouched (not an expense \u2014 the interest is the expense, the principal isn't). BS: cash \u22125,000, debt \u22125,000. CFS: \u22125,000 in financing."},
{q: "Rp2,000k of salaries are earned in December but paid in January. December treatment?", opts: ["Not recorded \u2014 not yet paid","IS: salary expense \u22122,000; BS: salaries payable +2,000 (accrual)","CFS: operating \u22122,000","BS: cash \u22122,000"], answer: 1, k: "akuntansi", diff: "Medium",
 hints: ["Accrual: record when INCURRED.","Cash moves in January \u2014 December?"],
 explain: "Accrual: the expense is recognized when incurred (December) \u2014 IS \u22122,000, BS payable +2,000. Cash & CFS move only in January. 'Not paid = not recorded' = cash basis, not accrual."},
{q: "A Rp30,000k machine, 5-year life. A: straight line (6,000/yr). B: 40% declining balance (12,000 in year 1). Tax 25%. Year-1 CFO?", opts: ["B's CFO higher by 1,500 (tax shield)","CFO identical","A's CFO higher by 1,500","B's CFO lower"], answer: 0, k: "akuntansi", diff: "Hard",
 hints: ["Charge gap = 12,000 \u2212 6,000 = 6,000.","Tax shield = 6,000 \u00d7 25%."],
 explain: "B's bigger charge 6,000 \u2192 B's tax smaller by 6,000 \u00d7 25% = 1,500 \u2192 B's CFO higher by 1,500. That's the depreciation tax shield: without taxes methods don't move cash; with taxes, aggressive = more early cash."},
{q: "NI Rp15,000k, D&A Rp5,000k, working capital DOWN Rp1,000k. CFO?", opts: ["Rp21,000k","Rp19,000k","Rp20,000k","Rp9,000k"], answer: 0, k: "arus_kas", diff: "Medium",
 hints: ["WC down = cash released \u2192 ADD.","15,000 + 5,000 + 1,000 = ?"],
 explain: "15,000 + 5,000 + 1,000 = 21,000. Falling WC = cash freed from the working-capital 'prison' \u2014 positive sign. 19,000 = flipped sign (the 'WC is always subtracted' reflex)."},
{q: "A 2:1 stock split (each share becomes 2). The financial-statement trail?", opts: ["Equity doubles","No value change \u2014 just shares \u00d7 2 (price per share \u00f7 2); nothing hits the 3 statements","Cash rises","Retained earnings fall"], answer: 1, k: "akuntansi", diff: "Medium",
 hints: ["A split = cutting the pizza into more slices.","Total pizza = ?"],
 explain: "A split changes nothing of value \u2014 just denomination: shares \u00d72, price \u00f72. Nothing hits the 3 statements (footnote & share count only). Answering 'equity rises' confuses a split with a new share issue."},
{q: "Tax losses of Rp10,000k. Tax rate 22%. Recognizable deferred tax asset?", opts: ["Rp2,200k automatically","Rp2,200k ONLY if future taxable profits are probable; otherwise none (in full)","Rp10,000k","Zero \u2014 losses can't be assets"], answer: 1, k: "akuntansi", diff: "Hard",
 hints: ["10,000 \u00d7 22% = 2,200 = the ceiling.","DTA recognition condition for losses = ?"],
 explain: "2,200 is the ceiling, not an automatic right. Loss-based DTAs are recognized only when future taxable profits are probable \u2014 otherwise a valuation allowance (zero). 'Auto-multiply by the rate' = a phantom asset."},
{q: "NI 9,000, D&A 2,500, WC up 1,000, capex 3,000, dividends 2,000, beginning cash 3,000 (k). Which statement is WRONG?", opts: ["CFO = 10,500","Ending cash = 8,500","CFO = 13,500","Dividends sit in CFF"], answer: 2, k: "linkage", diff: "Hard",
 hints: ["CFO = 9,000 + 2,500 \u2212 1,000.","Ending cash = 3,000 + CFO \u2212 3,000 \u2212 2,000."],
 explain: "CFO = 10,500 (not 13,500 \u2014 13,500 = flipped WC sign). Ending cash = 3,000 + 10,500 \u2212 3,000 \u2212 2,000 = 8,500. Rising \u0394WC = subtract \u2014 in multi-line questions, the sign is what collapses first."},
{q: "Current assets Rp18,000k, current liabilities Rp11,000k. Working capital?", opts: ["Rp7,000k","Rp29,000k","Rp11,000k","Rp18,000k"], answer: 0, k: "modal_kerja", diff: "Easy",
 hints: ["18,000 \u2212 11,000 = ?","Write the numbers down, subtract carefully."],
 explain: "18,000 \u2212 11,000 = 7,000."},
{q: "Inventory Rp4,000k, annual COGS Rp36,000k (360 days). DIO?", opts: ["40 days","36 days","90 days","10 days"], answer: 0, k: "modal_kerja", diff: "Medium",
 hints: ["DIO = inventory \u00f7 COGS \u00d7 360.","Write the numbers down, subtract carefully."],
 explain: "4,000 \u00f7 36,000 \u00d7 360 = 40 days \u2014 inventory sits 40 days before selling. High DIO = cash parked in the warehouse."},
{q: "DIO 50 days, DSO 35 days, DPO 60 days. CCC?", opts: ["25 days","145 days","85 days","45 days"], answer: 0, k: "siklus_kas", diff: "Medium",
 hints: ["50 + 35 \u2212 60 = ?","DPO subtracts \u2014 don't flip it."],
 explain: "50 + 35 \u2212 60 = 25 days \u2014 suppliers (60 days) nearly fund the whole operating cycle (85 days). DPO > DIO+DSO = negative CCC = a supplier-financed business model."},
{q: "Receivables up 3,000, inventory up 1,000, payables up 2,500 (k). CFO impact?", opts: ["CFO down 1,500","CFO up 1,500","CFO down 4,000","Unchanged"], answer: 0, k: "modal_kerja", diff: "Hard",
 hints: ["\u0394WC = (3,000 + 1,000) \u2212 2,500.","CFO = NI + D&A \u2212 \u0394WC."],
 explain: "\u0394WC = 4,000 \u2212 2,500 = +1,500 \u2192 CFO down 1,500. Assets up 4,000 eat cash, payables up 2,500 return some \u2014 net 1,500 tied up."},
{q: "DPO extended 45 \u2192 60 days, annual purchases Rp72,000k. One-time cash released?", opts: ["Rp3,000k","Rp1,500k","Rp12,000k","Rp72,000k"], answer: 0, k: "modal_kerja", diff: "Easy",
 hints: ["72,000 \u00f7 360 = 200/day.","15 \u00d7 200 = ?"],
 explain: "72,000 \u00f7 360 = 200/day; 15 days \u00d7 200 = 3,000."},
{q: "NOPAT 10,000, D&A 3,000, capex 5,000, \u0394WC 1,500 (k). FCFF?", opts: ["6,500","8,000","4,500","11,500"], answer: 0, k: "dcf", diff: "Easy",
 hints: ["10,000 + 3,000 \u2212 5,000 \u2212 1,500 = ?","Write the formula first, then plug in numbers."],
 explain: "10,000 + 3,000 \u2212 5,000 \u2212 1,500 = 6,500."},
{q: "Rf 7%, beta 1.5, MRP 4%. Cost of equity?", opts: ["13%","11%","17%","7%"], answer: 0, k: "capm", diff: "Easy",
 hints: ["7% + 1.5 \u00d7 4% = ?","Beta < 1 = defensive; beta > 1 = aggressive."],
 explain: "7% + 6% = 13%."},
{q: "Equity 50, debt 50, Ke 13%, Kd 9%, tax 22%. WACC?", opts: ["10.01%","11%","9.50%","10.50%"], answer: 0, k: "wacc", diff: "Medium",
 hints: ["0.5\u00d713% + 0.5\u00d79%\u00d70.78 = ?","Check: WACC must sit below Ke."],
 explain: "0.5 \u00d7 13% + 0.5 \u00d7 9% \u00d7 0.78 = 6.5% + 3.51% = 10.01%. A 50/50 structure = WACC exactly midway between Ke and after-tax Kd."},
{q: "Year-3 FCFF Rp2,000k, WACC 10%. PV?", opts: ["Rp1,503k","Rp2,000k","Rp1,818k","Rp1,653k"], answer: 0, k: "dcf", diff: "Medium",
 hints: ["PV = 2,000 \u00f7 1.1\u00b3.","1.1\u00b3 = 1.331."],
 explain: "2,000 \u00f7 1.331 = 1,502.63 \u2248 1,503. 1,653 = discounted 2 years (wrong year \u2014 'read the question')."},
{q: "FCFF6 Rp4,400k, g 3%, WACC 11%. TV?", opts: ["Rp55,000k","Rp40,000k","Rp44,000k","Rp36,667k"], answer: 0, k: "tv", diff: "Medium",
 hints: ["4,400 \u00f7 (0.11 \u2212 0.03) = ?","Ask first: which year does the TV sit in?"],
 explain: "4,400 \u00f7 0.08 = 55,000."},
{q: "Year-5 EBITDA Rp8,000k, exit multiple 7.5x. TV?", opts: ["Rp60,000k","Rp56,000k","Rp64,000k","Rp53,333k"], answer: 0, k: "tv", diff: "Medium",
 hints: ["8,000 \u00d7 7.5 = ?","Ask first: which year does the TV sit in?"],
 explain: "8,000 \u00d7 7.5 = 60,000."},
{q: "EV Rp120,000k, debt Rp35,000k, cash Rp8,000k, 15,500 shares. Value per share?", opts: ["Rp6,000","Rp5,484","Rp7,742","Rp6,516"], answer: 0, k: "dcf", diff: "Hard",
 hints: ["Equity = 120,000 \u2212 35,000 + 8,000.","93,000 \u00f7 15,500 = ?"],
 explain: "Equity = 93,000; \u00f7 15,500 = 6,000/share. 5,484 = forgot to add cash. At big numbers, one forgotten bridge = 516/share off."},
{q: "TV Rp55,000k at end of year 5, WACC 11%. PV of TV?", opts: ["Rp32,641k","Rp55,000k","Rp49,550k","Rp29,429k"], answer: 0, k: "tv", diff: "Hard",
 hints: ["PV = 55,000 \u00f7 1.11^5.","1.11^5 \u2248 1.6851."],
 explain: "55,000 \u00f7 1.6851 = 32,641. 49,550 = discounted 1 year; 29,429 = 6 years."},
{q: "Why does standard DCF use FCFF (unlevered) instead of net income?", opts: ["Net income is always wrong","FCFF measures operating cash before financing decisions \u2014 interest is a financing decision, not operations; whole-firm valuation must be capital-structure independent","FCFF is bigger","It's a rule"], answer: 1, k: "dcf", diff: "Hard",
 hints: ["Interest = the price of debt = a financing decision.","DCF values the FIRM, not the equity."],
 explain: "Net income is already after interest \u2014 then discounting at WACC (which also embeds debt) = double-counting debt (once in cash flows, once in the discount rate). Unlevered FCFF + WACC = capital-structure-independent valuation."},
{q: "TV = 62% of EV in a DCF. Implication?", opts: ["The model is wrong","Normal \u2014 but 62% of the valuation rests on WACC & g assumptions: validating them is the most critical job","Ignore the TV","Raise g"], answer: 1, k: "tv", diff: "Medium",
 hints: ["62% > half the valuation.","Where's the biggest risk?"],
 explain: "TV at 60\u201380% of EV is normal \u2014 but it means over half the 'valuation' is really a bet on two numbers: WACC and g. The best validation hours go there, not to the year-2 forecast."},
{q: "Market cap Rp60,000k, debt Rp15,000k, cash Rp7,000k. EV?", opts: ["Rp68,000k","Rp60,000k","Rp82,000k","Rp53,000k"], answer: 0, k: "comps", diff: "Easy",
 hints: ["60,000 + 15,000 \u2212 7,000 = ?","EV first, then bridge to equity."],
 explain: "60,000 + 15,000 \u2212 7,000 = 68,000."},
{q: "Peer EV/EBITDA 10x. EBITDA Rp9,000k, net debt Rp12,000k, 13,000 shares. Value per share?", opts: ["Rp6,000","Rp6,923","Rp5,077","Rp7,692"], answer: 0, k: "multiples", diff: "Medium",
 hints: ["EV = 90,000; equity = 90,000 \u2212 12,000.","78,000 \u00f7 13,000 = ?"],
 explain: "EV = 10 \u00d7 9,000 = 90,000; equity = 78,000; \u00f7 13,000 = 6,000/share."},
{q: "LTM vs NTM multiples: the difference and the trap?", opts: ["Same thing","LTM = trailing 12-month denominator (factual); NTM = next-12-month forecast. Trap: mixing the target's LTM with peers' NTM","NTM is always higher","LTM must never be used"], answer: 1, k: "multiples", diff: "Medium",
 hints: ["LTM = Last Twelve Months (already happened).","Compare apples to apples: denominator periods must match."],
 explain: "LTM is factual vs NTM is expected \u2014 different things. Comparing the target's LTM EV/EBITDA (8x) with peers' NTM (10x) = mixing fact with hope. Align the basis first."},
{q: "Comps EV Rp68,000k. A comparable precedent paid a 25% control premium. Precedent EV?", opts: ["Rp85,000k","Rp68,000k","Rp51,000k","Rp93,000k"], answer: 0, k: "comps", diff: "Medium",
 hints: ["68,000 \u00d7 1.25 = ?","EV first, then bridge to equity."],
 explain: "68,000 \u00d7 1.25 = 85,000."},
{q: "Data table: WACC 10\u201314%, g 1\u20135%. Highest EV at WACC 10% + g 5%. Why?", opts: ["Miscalculated","The (WACC\u2212g) denominator = 0.05, the smallest \u2192 largest TV","Lower WACC raises FCFF","Higher g cuts capex"], answer: 1, k: "skenario", diff: "Easy",
 hints: ["0.10 \u2212 0.05 = 0.05.","One number = one world; find the range."],
 explain: "The 0.05 denominator is smallest \u2192 largest TV. At this point sensitivity goes wild: g 5%\u21926% (0.04 denominator) lifts TV 25% \u2014 from 1 point of assumption."},
{q: "Tornado: the 'capex' bar is shortest, the 'exit multiple' bar longest. Allocation call?", opts: ["Focus on capex","Focus on the exit multiple; capex gets minimal time","Split evenly","Ignore the chart"], answer: 1, k: "sensitivitas", diff: "Medium",
 hints: ["Longest = bites hardest.","Longest bar = your time allocation."],
 explain: "The longest exit-multiple bar = validating comparable multiples is job #1. The stubby capex bar = accept the number, don't spend the session there. The tornado = the analyst-hours budget."},
{q: "Base EV Rp70,000k, bear Rp48,000k. Market price Rp55,000k. Thesis: 'buy, base beats market'?", opts: ["Valid \u2014 base beats market","Fragile: in the bear case (48,000 < 55,000) margin of safety is NEGATIVE \u2014 the thesis only lives if the bear never happens","Buy more","Sell"], answer: 1, k: "skenario", diff: "Hard",
 hints: ["Bear 48,000 vs paying 55,000 = ?","Margin of safety measured from which scenario?"],
 explain: "In the bear case you pay 55,000 for 48,000 of value = a 7,000 loss. A 'base > market' thesis is fragile when the bear has material probability. Honest margin of safety is measured from the bear, not the base."},
{q: "In the Day-17 LBO model (TV \u2248 70% of EV, debt repaid by year 5): sensitivity of exit multiple \u00b11x vs growth \u00b12%. Which bites EV harder, and why?", opts: ["Growth \u2014 always","The exit multiple \u2014 it bites directly through TV at 70% of EV; growth works via 5-year compounding with more spread-out effects","Equal","Can't compare"], answer: 1, k: "sensitivitas", diff: "Hard",
 hints: ["TV = 70% of EV; the multiple multiplies TV directly.","Growth works through 5 years of compounding."],
 explain: "A \u00b11x exit multiple on 70%-of-EV TV = \u00b1~10% EV swing directly. \u00b12% growth spreads through 5-year compounding + deleveraging \u2014 big too, but a less direct channel. This holds for this model; with different leverage/structure the ranking can flip \u2014 always run your own sensitivity, never memorize rankings."},
{q: "Fixed cost Rp24,000k, price Rp1,200k/unit, variable cost Rp800k/unit. BEP?", opts: ["60 units","20 units","30 units","48 units"], answer: 0, k: "skenario", diff: "Easy",
 hints: ["24,000 \u00f7 (1,200 \u2212 800) = ?","One number = one world; find the range."],
 explain: "24,000 \u00f7 400 = 60 units."},
{q: "A acquires B entirely with shares. A's P/E 8x, B's P/E 14x. Year 1 (ignore synergies)?", opts: ["Accretive","Dilutive \u2014 buying 14x earnings with 8x 'currency'","Neutral","Need debt info"], answer: 1, k: "ma", diff: "Easy",
 hints: ["Pay 14x for earnings your market prices at 8x.","Cheap or expensive?"],
 explain: "Dilutive: each new share 'buys' less earnings than it dilutes \u2014 you pay dear (14x) with cheap currency (8x). The mirror image of classic accretion."},
{q: "An all-cash deal funded by debt @5%. Target P/E 14x, tax rate 22%. Accretion/dilution (ignore synergies)?", opts: ["Always dilutive \u2014 there's interest","Accretive: the target's 7.14% earnings yield > 3.9% after-tax debt cost","Neutral","Cannot be determined"], answer: 1, k: "ma", diff: "Medium",
 hints: ["Earnings yield = 1 \u00f7 14.","After-tax debt cost = 5% \u00d7 0.78."],
 explain: "Earnings yield 1/14 = 7.14% vs after-tax debt cost 5% \u00d7 (1\u22120.22) = 3.9%. The earnings bought (7.14%) exceed the funding cost (3.9%) \u2192 accretive. Quick cash-deal rule: compare yield vs after-tax cost of debt."},
{q: "Paid Rp50,000k for target equity with book value Rp30,000k (assume = fair value of identifiable net assets). Goodwill and treatment?", opts: ["Goodwill 20,000; amortized over 10 years","Goodwill 20,000; NOT amortized \u2014 annual impairment test","Not goodwill","Goodwill 30,000"], answer: 1, k: "ma", diff: "Medium",
 hints: ["Goodwill = price \u2212 fair value of net assets.","Standard: goodwill is not depreciated."],
 explain: "Goodwill = 50,000 \u2212 30,000 = 20,000 (valid only under the stated assumption). General IFRS 3 rule: goodwill = price \u2212 FAIR VALUE of identifiable net assets, not price \u2212 book value. Not amortized \u2014 impairment-tested yearly. 'Amortize over 10 years' = a dead rule from stale sources; it traps learners of outdated material."},
{q: "The target has Rp12,000k NOLs + Rp3,000k/yr cost synergies from year 2. The interaction?", opts: ["No interaction","Synergies raise taxable profit \u2192 accelerate NOL use \u2192 the NOLs are worth more to THIS acquirer than to one without synergies","NOLs reduce synergies","Both expire"], answer: 1, k: "ma", diff: "Hard",
 hints: ["NOLs need profits to be used.","Synergies = extra profit."],
 explain: "Cross-topic interaction: NOLs are only worth something with taxable profits; the 3,000/yr synergies create those profits \u2192 faster NOL use \u2192 higher PV of tax savings. NOL value is NOT buyer-independent \u2014 a synergistic buyer pays more for the same NOLs."},
{q: "EV Rp60,000k + fee Rp1,500k. TL Rp36,000k + mezzanine Rp5,000k. Equity?", opts: ["Rp20,500k","Rp19,000k","Rp25,500k","Rp61,500k"], answer: 0, k: "lbo", diff: "Medium",
 hints: ["Uses = 61,500.","61,500 \u2212 36,000 \u2212 5,000 = ?"],
 explain: "Uses = 61,500; equity = 61,500 \u2212 36,000 \u2212 5,000 = 20,500. Two debt layers \u2014 subtract BOTH. 19,000 = forgot the fee."},
{q: "Interest: TL Rp36,000k @8% + mezzanine Rp5,000k @12%. Total year-1 interest?", opts: ["Rp3,480k","Rp2,880k","Rp4,080k","Rp3,080k"], answer: 0, k: "lbo", diff: "Medium",
 hints: ["36,000 \u00d7 8% + 5,000 \u00d7 12%.","Check sources = uses before moving on."],
 explain: "2,880 + 600 = 3,480. Each tranche has its rate \u2014 sum them, don't average. 2,880 = forgot the mezzanine."},
{q: "TL Rp36,000k, 10%/year mandatory amortization. Cash available to debt Rp5,000k. Payment?", opts: ["3,600 mandatory + 1,400 optional (sweep)","All 5,000 mandatory","3,600 only, hold the rest","Pay nothing"], answer: 0, k: "lbo", diff: "Hard",
 hints: ["Mandatory = 10% \u00d7 36,000.","Cash left after mandatory = ?"],
 explain: "Mandatory = 3,600; the 1,400 remainder = optional prepayment (cash sweep). '3,600 only' wastes deleveraging \u2014 cash idles while 8% interest runs. LBO structure: mandatory first, sweep the rest."},
{q: "Entry equity Rp20,500k \u2192 exit Rp62,000k (5 years). MoIC?", opts: ["3.02x","2.02x","4.02x","3.52x"], answer: 0, k: "lbo", diff: "Hard",
 hints: ["62,000 \u00f7 20,500 = 3.0244.","Check sources = uses before moving on."],
 explain: "62,000 \u00f7 20,500 = 3.02x \u2192 202% gain. Quick check: 20,500 \u00d7 3 = 61,500 \u2248 62,000 \u2713."},
{q: "Dividend recap: Rp8,000k of new debt drawn for a sponsor dividend in year 3. MoIC & IRR impact?", opts: ["Both MoIC and IRR rise \u2014 earlier cash back","MoIC UNCHANGED (same total cash back; only timing shifts) \u2014 IRR rises; leverage & risk rise","MoIC falls; IRR flat","No impact on either"], answer: 1, k: "lbo", diff: "Hard",
 hints: ["MoIC = total cash back \u00f7 capital \u2014 timing is not in the formula.","IRR rewards time: earlier cash = higher IRR."],
 explain: "MoIC is blind to timing: the 8,000 dividend in year 3 + a smaller exit proceed (the 8,000 of debt is still on the company) = the SAME total cash to sponsor \u2192 MoIC unchanged (before interest/fees on the new debt). What rises is IRR \u2014 cash comes back earlier. Lesson: MoIC without a timeline is half the story; recaps shift risk to lenders."},
{q: "The role of a flexible budget in variance analysis?", opts: ["Recasts the budget at actual volume, separating price variance from volume variance","Raises the budget","Replaces the forecast","Deletes variances"], answer: 0, k: "budgeting", diff: "Easy",
 hints: ["Static budget vs actual volume = all mixed up.","Flex = budget RECAST at actual volume."],
 explain: "A flexible budget recasts the budget at actual volume. Without it, revenue variance mixes \u2018sold more\u2019 with \u2018sold dearer\u2019 \u2014 two different problems."},
{q: "Actual revenue \u22125% (unfav), costs \u22128% (fav) vs budget. Reading?", opts: ["Bad \u2014 revenue miss","Good \u2014 costs fell","Mixed: margin improved (costs fell more) BUT the revenue miss needs investigation \u2014 don't celebrate before knowing why","Neutral"], answer: 2, k: "budgeting", diff: "Medium",
 hints: ["Costs \u22128% vs revenue \u22125% \u2192 margin?","Why did revenue fall? Volume? Price?"],
 explain: "Margin improved (costs down 8% > revenue down 5%) \u2014 but a 5% revenue miss is an alarm: is volume leaking to competitors? Don't celebrate efficiency before diagnosing the top line."},
{q: "Budget: 5,000 units @Rp200k. Actual: 4,600 units, revenue Rp897,000k. Price & volume variance?", opts: ["Price \u221223,000 (unfav); volume \u221280,000 (unfav)","Price +23,000 (fav); volume +80,000","Both zero","Volume only"], answer: 0, k: "budgeting", diff: "Hard",
 hints: ["Flex = 4,600 \u00d7 200 = 920,000.","Price var = 897,000 \u2212 920,000."],
 explain: "Flex = 920,000. Price variance = 897,000 \u2212 920,000 = \u221223,000 unfav (sold at 195,000/unit vs 200,000 budget). Volume variance = 920,000 \u2212 1,000,000 = \u221280,000 unfav (400 units lost). Both red \u2014 a double problem."},
{q: "October: the board needs a 2027 outlook but the 2026 budget is static. Fix, and why?", opts: ["Wait for January","Rolling forecast: a 12-month horizon stays fresh; a static budget in October is blind to 2027","Just guess","Extend the 2026 budget"], answer: 1, k: "forecast", diff: "Medium",
 hints: ["In October, how far does the 2026 budget see?","Rolling always sees 12 months ahead."],
 explain: "A quarterly-refreshed rolling forecast always sees 12 months ahead \u2014 October 2026 already 'sees' into September 2027. The static budget is blind. 'Wait for January' = 3 months with no steering."},
{q: "The formula =$D$10*E5 is copied right. What happens?", opts: ["D10 locked, E5 \u2192 F5, G5 (relative)","Both locked","Both shift","Error"], answer: 0, k: "excel", diff: "Easy",
 hints: ["$D$10 = fully absolute.","E5 = fully relative."],
 explain: "$D$10 stays; E5 \u2192 F5 \u2192 G5. The classic pattern: rate/assumption in column D (locked), running data in row 5 (flowing)."},
{q: "Repeated VLOOKUP #N/A though the data exists. The tidiest fix?", opts: ["Retype the data","=IFERROR(XLOOKUP(lookup,range,return),\"check data\") \u2014 plus check spaces/data types in the lookup value","Ignore it","Use HLOOKUP"], answer: 1, k: "excel", diff: "Medium",
 hints: ["#N/A often = hidden spaces / text-vs-number.","IFERROR for a tidy message; XLOOKUP for exact default."],
 explain: "The most common #N/A causes: trailing spaces ('2025 ' vs '2025') or text-vs-number. IFERROR gives a tidy message; XLOOKUP's exact-default avoids VLOOKUP's approximate trap. Fix the source, don't mask the error."},
{q: "Total sales on 2 criteria (branch + month). The formula?", opts: ["=SUMIFS(Sales,Branch,\"Jakarta\",Month,\"Jan\")","=SUM(Sales)","=VLOOKUP(\"Jakarta\",Table,5)","=COUNTIFS(...)"], answer: 0, k: "excel", diff: "Medium",
 hints: ["SUMIFS = sum with MANY criteria.","Order: sum_range, criteria1_range, criteria1, ..."],
 explain: "=SUMIFS(Sales, Branch, \"Jakarta\", Month, \"Jan\"). COUNTIFS counts rows (doesn't sum values) \u2014 swapping them = a totally wrong answer."},
{q: "=LET(g,B2, rev*(1+g)^5). The advantage over writing it raw?", opts: ["None","Readability & audit: g defined once, used many times \u2014 change in one place, consistent everywhere","Faster calc","Mandatory for models"], answer: 1, k: "excel", diff: "Hard",
 hints: ["How many times is g used in a long formula?","Change the assumption = change in how many places?"],
 explain: "LET names variables inside the formula: g defined once at B2, used repeatedly without layered $ refs. Change one place \u2014 consistent. For long 5-year formulas, this separates auditable models from unauditable ones."},
{q: "A circular LBO model: interest on ending debt \u2192 iterative calculation switched on 'to make it run'. Your call?", opts: ["OK \u2014 it produces results","Reject: fix to beginning-debt interest; iterative masks a fragile circular that may not converge","Add more iterations","Shrink the interest"], answer: 1, k: "excel", diff: "Hard",
 hints: ["Iterative = Excel guessing until 'close enough'.","A circular that 'runs' \u2260 a circular that's right."],
 explain: "Iterative calculation covers up the circular reference instead of fixing it \u2014 it can fail to converge or converge wrongly with no warning. Standard: beginning-debt interest = no circular at all."}
],
hell: [
{q: "A DCF subtracts interest expense from FCFF. What's wrong?", opts: ["Nothing wrong","Wrong: FCFF is unlevered \u2014 interest is a financing decision, not operations. Subtracting it double-counts debt (in cash flows AND in WACC)","Interest should be added","FCFF doesn't exist"], answer: 1,
 explain: "Net income minus interest, discounted at WACC (which also embeds debt) = debt counted twice. FCFF = before interest; WACC handles financing. One door, not two."},
{q: "A circular LBO model (interest on ending debt) but 'the results look sensible'. Usable?", opts: ["Yes","No: fragile; iteration may not converge or be accurate; standard = beginning-debt interest","Yes if small","Yes if audited"], answer: 1,
 explain: "'Looks sensible' isn't validation. Circular + iterative = a model that can break when assumptions shift \u2014 exactly when you need it most (sensitivity). Fix the structure."},
{q: "Rp20,000k of goodwill amortized over 10 years. What's wrong?", opts: ["Nothing","Wrong: goodwill is NOT amortized \u2014 annual impairment test. Amortization = a dead rule no longer in force","Should be 5 years","No goodwill exists"], answer: 1,
 explain: "Current standards: goodwill isn't depreciated; it's impairment-tested yearly. Amortizing it = a fictitious 2,000/yr charge shrinking profit \u2014 from stale learning sources."},
{q: "Base case above market price, bear case below. Buy?", opts: ["Buy \u2014 base wins","It depends: bear < market price = negative margin of safety in the bad scenario. Buy only if the bear is unlikely AND you're paid for the risk","Sell","Wait"], answer: 1,
 explain: "No blind 'buy/sell' answer. Bear below market = you lose if the world turns bad. Decision = probability \u00d7 payoff \u2014 an honest thesis states when it dies."},
{q: "=SEQUENCE(10) hits filled cells \u2192 #SPILL!. The fix?", opts: ["Delete the formula","Clear/move the spill range \u2014 don't hand-type 10 numbers (back to the old way)","Ignore the error","Switch to VLOOKUP"], answer: 1,
 explain: "The fix = make room for the spill (clear/move), not ditch dynamic arrays. Hand-typing 10 numbers = back to an unauditable model."}
],
tasks: [
{t: "Task A \u2014 Mock Exam 3 (final), exam conditions (\u00b13 hrs)", d: "Dress rehearsal: click Start, 50 questions, no breaks, no notes. Treat it like the real Final Gate. Training target 80%+."},
{t: "Task B \u2014 Final debrief (\u00b12 hrs)", d: "Classify each trap (calculation/concept/misread). Flag traps appearing in 2+ mocks \u2014 those are your lifelong enemies; put them on the cheat sheet."},
{t: "Task C \u2014 Final Gate readiness checklist", d: "Fill the checklist above. Incomplete \u2192 Day 29 is the last repair + personal cheat sheet. Complete \u2192 consolidate, then Day 30."}
]
};
EN["hari-29"] = {
id: "hari-29",
track: "30 Days",
title: "FINAL REPAIR + 3-Hour Exam Strategy",
sub: "Day 29 \u00b7 \u00b13 hrs \u00b7 2-pass time management, 12 exam traps, exam-day ritual, cheatsheet",
est: "3 hours",
prerequisites: ["mock_exam"],
audio: "assets/audio/hari-29.mp3",
sections: [
{h: "3-hour time management: the 2-pass rule", body: `
<p>180 minutes, 50 questions \u2192 <b>\u00b13.5 minutes per question</b>. That is not advice \u2014 it is a budget. The easiest and hardest questions weigh the same; 10 minutes on 1 hard question = 3 easy questions sacrificed. The rule: <b>2 passes</b>.</p>
<table class="brutal">
<tr><th>Phase</th><th>Time</th><th>Rule</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Pass 1</b></td><td>90 minutes</td><td>Answer what you're sure of in &lt;2 min. If unsure: <b>flag, move on</b>. Don't bleed out in pass 1.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Pass 2</b></td><td>60 minutes</td><td>Return to flagged ones. Now solve with safe time remaining.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Check</b></td><td>30 minutes</td><td>Scan for traps (the 12 below) + make sure <b>everything is answered</b> \u2014 blank = certain 0.</td></tr>
</table>
<p>By minute 90, you must have touched all 50 questions at least once. That is the non-negotiable pass-1 target.</p>
<div class="callout"><b>Why is 'flag, move on' so hard to do?</b> Because of ego: 'almost got it'. But 8 minutes on 1 question = 2.3 other questions sacrificed. Flagging discipline = respecting the 3.5-minute budget you set yourself.</div>`},
{h: "12 exam question traps", body: `
<p>Twelve patterns that eat exam points \u2014 all appeared in the mocks. Memorize the patterns, not the questions:</p>
<table class="brutal">
<tr><th>#</th><th>Trap</th><th>Counter</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>The word <b>'except' / 'not'</b></td><td>Read the negation FIRST before the options; circle the word</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Decoy numbers (unused data)</td><td>Write the formula first \u2192 take only the numbers needed</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>Units: <b>Rp thousand vs Rp</b></td><td>Equalize units BEFORE computing, note it on scratch paper</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>'All are correct' option</td><td>Verify each option one by one; one wrong = option dead</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>Comma vs period decimals</td><td>2,535 vs 2.535 \u2014 read the number format</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>Twin options, one digit apart (4,791 vs 4,971)</td><td>Recompute fully, never 'probably'</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>TV forgetting \u00d7(1+g)</td><td>Write the full formula first: FCFF\u00d7(1+g)\u00f7(WACC\u2212g)</td></tr>
<tr><td>8</td><td>Tax on EBIT (not EBT)</td><td>Chain: EBIT \u2212 interest = EBT; tax = EBT \u00d7 rate</td></tr>
<tr><td>9</td><td>DSO with 365 / forgetting \u00f7360</td><td>Always: revenue \u00f7 360 first = daily revenue</td></tr>
<tr><td>10</td><td>MoIC read as a percent</td><td>2.8x = 180% gain (subtract 1 first)</td></tr>
<tr><td>11</td><td>'Cannot be determined' as the lazy answer</td><td>Check: is info truly missing, or did you give up?</td></tr>
<tr><td>12</td><td>Chain computed out of order</td><td>Follow the order: input \u2192 intermediate \u2192 output; no jumping</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="callout"><b>Why are the last 30 minutes = 'trap scan'?</b> Because a tired brain = traps 1\u20136 deadliest at minutes 150\u2013180. A final scan with this list catches 2\u20133 'knew it but misread it' questions \u2014 the cheapest points in the whole exam.</div>`},
{h: "The H-1 & exam-day ritual", body: `
<p>A 3-hour exam = a cognitive marathon. Physical prep decides \u00b110% of the score \u2014 and 10% = the pass/fail gap:</p>
<table class="brutal">
<tr><th>When</th><th>Ritual</th><th>Why</th></tr>
<tr><td>H-1 afternoon</td><td>Last touch of material: read the 12-trap bank 1\u00d7</td><td>After that the brain needs consolidation, not new input</td></tr>
<tr><td>H-1 night</td><td>Sleep 7\u20138 hours. <b>No all-nighters.</b></td><td>All-nighters damage recall 20\u201330%; 1 hour of panic cramming &lt; enough sleep</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exam morning</td><td>Normal breakfast + water. No food experiments.</td><td>Stable blood sugar = stable focus for 3 hours</td></tr>
<tr><td>Departure</td><td>Arrive 30 minutes early; bring calculator, ID, water</td><td>Logistics panic = the first 15 minutes destroyed</td></tr>
<tr><td>First 10 minutes</td><td><b>Write the cheatsheet from memory</b> on scratch paper</td><td>A stressed brain forgets 'memorized' formulas \u2014 paper = external memory</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="callout"><b>Why write the cheatsheet BEFORE starting the questions?</b> Because at minute 100 with 20 questions left, you won't recall the TV formula calmly. Writing it at minute 0 = locking every formula while the brain is fresh. It's legal \u2014 scratch paper is provided for exactly that.</div>`},
{h: "The 1-page cheatsheet: write it from memory", body: `
<p>Your cheatsheet template \u2014 10 formulas answering 80% of calculation questions. Your task: <b>write them all from memory</b>, compare with the template, repeat until 100% match:</p>
<table class="brutal">
<tr><th>#</th><th>Formula</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>TV = FCFF\u00d7(1+g) \u00f7 (WACC \u2212 g)</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>WACC = wE\u00d7ke + wD\u00d7kd\u00d7(1\u2212t)</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>Ending RE = beginning RE + NI \u2212 dividends</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>CFO = NI + depreciation \u00b1 \u0394WC</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>DSO = receivables \u00f7 (revenue \u00f7 360)</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>CCC = DSO + DIO \u2212 DPO</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>MoIC = exit equity \u00f7 initial equity</td></tr>
<tr><td>8</td><td>Interest = rate \u00d7 BEGINNING debt</td></tr>
<tr><td>9</td><td>Ending debt = MAX(beginning debt \u2212 cash, 0)</td></tr>
<tr><td>10</td><td>EV = equity + debt \u2212 cash</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Standard: write 10/10 correctly from memory, 2\u00d7 in a row. If one formula is still 'probably', that = 3 exam questions gambled.</p>
<div class="callout"><b>Why from memory, not a photocopy?</b> Because the exam allows no cheat sheets \u2014 the only thing you may bring in is what's in your head. A cheatsheet written from memory = proof the formulas truly live in your long-term memory.</div>`}
],
quiz: [
{q: "Exam: 180 minutes, 50 questions. Budget per question?", opts: ["\u00b12 min \u2014 the rest as reserve", "\u00b13.5 min \u2014 180 \u00f7 50", "\u00b15 min \u2014 the rest for checking", "Free, just finish"], answer: 1,
 hints: ["180 \u00f7 50 = ?", "A budget = a limit, not a target."],
 explain: "3.6 minutes \u2014 rounded to 3.5. This is a budget, not a target: easy questions under 2 minutes fund the hard ones. With no budget, 1 hard question eats 3 easy ones."},
{q: "Pass 1: a question feels like 8 minutes. Action?", opts: ["Finish it \u2014 already halfway", "Guess fast so it's not blank", "Flag it, move on \u2014 return in pass 2", "Skip permanently"], answer: 2,
 hints: ["8 minutes = how many question budgets?", "Ego says 'almost there'. What does the budget say?"],
 explain: "Flag, move on. 8 minutes = 2.3 question budgets sacrificed for 1 point. In pass 2 you return with safe time left \u2014 and the answer often surfaces after the brain 'rests' from that question."},
{q: "Question: 'which is NOT a trait of a budget?' Strategy?", opts: ["Read the word 'NOT' first \u2014 circle it \u2014 then eliminate options that ARE budget traits", "Read options first, then the question", "Pick the longest one", "Pick 'all are correct'"], answer: 0,
 hints: ["Trap #1: negation.", "A tired brain reads 'not' as 'is'."],
 explain: "Circle the negation BEFORE reading the options. Past minute 150, a tired brain turns 'not' into 'is' \u2014 1 second of circling saves 1 point."},
{q: "Data: revenue Rp54,000,000 (in Rp), receivables Rp6,000 (in Rp thousand). DSO?", opts: ["0.04 days \u2014 units not equalized", "40 days \u2014 equalize units first: receivables = 6,000 thousand = 6,000,000", "4 days \u2014 decimal slipped", "400 days \u2014 revenue not converted"], answer: 1,
 hints: ["Trap #3: units.", "6,000 thousand = ? rupiah."],
 explain: "Receivables 6,000 thousand = 6,000,000; revenue 54,000,000 \u2192 daily 150,000 \u2192 DSO 40 days. Unconverted = 0.04 days \u2014 an answer 'computed right, read wrong'. Write the conversion on scratch BEFORE computing."},
{q: "An 'all answers are correct' option. How to check?", opts: ["Pick it directly \u2014 usually right", "Never pick it", "Verify each option one by one; one wrong option = this option dead", "Pick the shortest"], answer: 2,
 hints: ["Trap #4.", "'All correct' = 4 claims, not 1."],
 explain: "'All correct' is 4 claims to verify one by one. One wrong option = the whole option dead. Fast way: find ONE clearly wrong option \u2014 found = eliminated."},
{q: "Options: 4,791 vs 4,971. You computed 4,791. Action?", opts: ["Pick 4,791 \u2014 'probably right'", "Pick 4,971 \u2014 the different one is usually right", "Guess", "Recompute fully from the formula \u2014 twin options are designed to catch digit slips"], answer: 3,
 hints: ["Trap #6: twin options.", "'Probably' is not a standard."],
 explain: "Twin options one digit apart = a trap for transposition slips (4,791 vs 4,971). Recompute fully \u2014 60 seconds of verification is cheaper than 1 point lost to 'probably'."},
{q: "15 minutes left, 10 questions still blank. Action?", opts: ["Focus finishing 3 correctly, leave the rest blank", "Answer ALL \u2014 blank = certain 0; smart-guess via elimination per question", "Panic and click randomly", "Ask for extra time"], answer: 1,
 hints: ["Blank = 0% chance correct.", "Smart guess after eliminating 1 option = 33%."],
 explain: "Blank = certain 0. Smart guessing (eliminate 1\u20132 options first) = 33\u201350% per question. 10 questions \u00d7 33% \u2248 3 free points \u2014 possibly the passing margin."},
{q: "When to write the cheatsheet on scratch paper?", opts: ["Not needed \u2014 memorized in head is enough", "Minute 100, when formulas are forgotten", "After the exam", "First 10 minutes, from memory, before touching any question"], answer: 3,
 hints: ["When is the brain freshest?", "Minute-100 stress vs minute-0 calm."],
 explain: "Minutes 0\u201310: fresh brain, write 10 formulas from memory = external memory for the next 170 minutes. Waiting until you forget = the formulas are gone when needed most."},
{q: "H-1: 3 extra hours cramming vs 8 hours sleep. Pick?", opts: ["Cram \u2014 3 hours of memorizing > sleep", "Cram + 3 coffees", "Sleep 8 hours \u2014 all-nighters damage recall 20\u201330%; panic cramming never reaches long-term memory", "No sleep at all"], answer: 2,
 hints: ["An exam = recall, not input.", "When does memory consolidation happen?"],
 explain: "Sleep = when the brain consolidates memory. All-nighters damage recall 20\u201330% \u2014 trading 3 hours of panic cramming (never stored) for 20% of the whole exam. Not a good trade."},
{q: "A question gives 6 numbers; the TV formula needs 3. Action?", opts: ["Use all 6 \u2014 more complete = more correct", "Average all 6 numbers", "Pick the 3 biggest", "Write the formula first \u2192 take only what's needed; the rest are decoys"], answer: 3,
 hints: ["Trap #2: decoy numbers.", "The formula = a needs filter."],
 explain: "Formula written first = you know exactly the 3 numbers needed; the other 3 = decoys designed to be used. 'Use everything' = the answer the setter cooked for those who skip writing the formula."}
],
hell: [
{q: "Pass 1 overruns: finished at minute 100 (planned 90). Adjustment?", opts: ["Rush pass 2 in 50 minutes with no final check", "Cut: pass 2 = 50 minutes, check = full 30 \u2014 the final check must not be sacrificed", "Redo pass 1 for the doubtful ones", "Give up on the 10 hardest questions"], answer: 1,
 explain: "Never sacrifice the 30-minute check \u2014 that's where 2\u20133 'knew it but misread it' points are rescued. Compress pass 2 (60\u219250), keep the full check. Budget discipline = cutting the cheapest, not the most valuable."},
{q: "Two options both feel right. Tiebreak?", opts: ["Pick the first that came to mind", "Look for absolute words ('always', 'certainly', 'only', 'never') \u2014 absolute claims are usually wrong", "Pick the longer one", "Flip a coin"], answer: 1,
 explain: "Absolute claims ('X always raises Y') are easiest to break with one counterexample \u2014 setters know it. Between two 'feels right' options, the qualified one ('tends to', 'can') is usually the designed-correct one."},
{q: "FCFF 3,000, g 2%, WACC 9%. TV? (beware trap #7)", opts: ["Rp42,857k \u2014 3,000 \u00f7 0.07, forgot \u00d7(1+g)", "Rp33,333k \u2014 3,000 \u00f7 0.09", "Rp43,714k \u2014 3,000\u00d71.02 \u00f7 0.07", "Rp30,600k \u2014 3,060 \u00f7 0.10"], answer: 2,
 explain: "3,060 \u00f7 0.07 = 43,714. Trap #7 claims victims even in hell mode: 42,857 looks 'almost right' \u2014 a gap of 857 = 1 point lost to one skipped multiplication."},
{q: "Your friend's cheatsheet says: 'WACC = wE\u00d7ke + wD\u00d7kd'. The error?", opts: ["None \u2014 already correct", "Missing (1\u2212t): debt \u00d7 (1\u2212tax); without it WACC is 1\u20132 points high and TV misses badly", "WACC needs no memorizing", "Missing parentheses"], answer: 1,
 explain: "Without (1\u2212t), WACC = 11.6% not 10.9% (Day 24 example) \u2192 TV denominator grows \u2192 valuation sinks. One missing factor on the cheatsheet = 5 exam questions systematically wrong. That's why the cheatsheet standard is 10/10, not 9/10."}
],
tasks: [
{t: "Task A \u2014 Write the cheatsheet from memory", d: "Close the lesson, write all 10 formulas on paper. Compare with the template. Repeat until 10/10 correct, 2\u00d7 in a row."},
{t: "Task B \u2014 Timed 10-question simulation", d: "Do the 10 quiz questions above in 35 minutes (3.5 min/question): pass 1 flags the doubtful, pass 2 returns, 5 minutes of trap-checking."},
{t: "Task C \u2014 Your H-1 ritual checklist", d: "Write your H-1 & exam-day ritual (sleep, breakfast, gear, departure). Stick it somewhere visible."}
]
};
EN["hari-30"] = {
id: "hari-30",
track: "30 Days",
title: "FINAL GATE: FMVA Sprint Graduation",
sub: "Day 30 \u00b7 \u00b12 hrs \u00b7 Graduation verification + the road after",
est: "2 hours",
prerequisites: ["mock_exam","capstone","thesis"],
audio: "assets/audio/hari-30.mp3",
sections: [
{h: "The final gate rules", body: `
<p>Thirty days. Three gates. Three mocks. Two capstones. The final gate is not a new exam \u2014 it is a <b>verification</b> that all the evidence exists. Two requirements, both mandatory:</p>
<table class="brutal">
<tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Detail</th><th>Evidence</th></tr>
<tr><td>\ud83d\udd11 Mock \u226570%</td><td><b>At least 2</b> of the 3 mocks \u226570% (timed, honest exam conditions), with no major weak domain</td><td>Recorded score per mock (honest \u2014 from timed exam conditions)</td></tr>
<tr><td>\ud83d\udd11 2 capstones done</td><td>Capstone I (INDF, Days 12\u201313) + Capstone II (bank, Day 26), each graded via <b>self rubric</b></td><td>Model + completed rubric + 1 page of reflection per capstone</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The 70% bar = the real FMVA exam pass mark. Not 100 \u2014 because even professional analysts don't know everything; what matters is a <b>proven foundation</b>.</p>
<div class="callout"><b>Why at least 2 mocks, not 1?</b> Because a single pass can be a lucky day or memorized questions \u2014 not proof of ability. Two timed passes = a pattern. Plus the no-major-weak-domain rule: graduating is about having no big holes, not about the average.</div>`},
{h: "Graduation checklist", body: `
<p>Check them off one by one. Anything unchecked = today's work, not 'later':</p>
<table class="brutal">
<tr><th>\u2610</th><th>Item</th><th>Status</th></tr>
<tr><td>\u2610</td><td>Gates 1, 2, 3 passed (quiz + timed model)</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>\u2610</td><td>Mocks 1, 2, 3 done timed at 3 hours, scores recorded</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>\u2610</td><td>At least 2 mocks \u226570% (timed, honest), no major weak domain</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>\u2610</td><td>Capstone I (INDF): model + self rubric + 1-page reflection</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>\u2610</td><td>Capstone II (bank): model + self rubric + 1-page reflection</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>\u2610</td><td>Thesis defense: 10-minute voice note + rubric \u226512</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>\u2610</td><td>10-formula cheatsheet: 10/10 from memory</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>\u2610</td><td>12-trap bank: patterns memorized, not questions</td><td></td></tr>
</table>
<p>All checked = <b>GRADUATED</b>. Anything empty = go back to its day, finish it, then return here. A gate you can walk through without passing is decoration \u2014 that has been the rule since Day 7.</p>
<div class="callout"><b>Why is this checklist strict?</b> Because 'graduated' here means: you dare to defend every number in your two capstones in front of an interviewer. If one item is unchecked, the interviewer will find it in 5 minutes.</div>`},
{h: "\ud83c\udf93 After graduating: the roadmap", body: `
<p>Congratulations. You completed 30 days most people never attempt. This sprint certificate is not a finish line \u2014 it is an entry ticket. The roadmap ahead:</p>
<table class="brutal">
<tr><th>Phase</th><th>Focus</th><th>Concrete action</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Interview prep</b> (2\u20134 weeks)</td><td>Tell your model's story</td><td>'Walk me through your DCF' in 5 minutes: assumptions \u2192 FCFF \u2192 WACC \u2192 TV \u2192 sensitivity. Prepare answers for the Day-27 15-question bank.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Portfolio</b></td><td>Polish the 2 capstones</td><td>One 1-page case study per capstone: thesis, model, result, lesson. Only ones whose every number you can defend.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Next skills</b></td><td>Automation + credit</td><td>Python for screening &amp; data (pandas), then credit analysis: bank financials deeper than Capstone II.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Keeping the skill</b></td><td>1 model per month</td><td>Take 1 real issuer per month, build a 1-day DCF. Modeling skill = muscle: unused = shrinking.</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="callout">\ud83c\udf93 <b>Why '1 model per month'?</b> Because the 30-day sprint built the foundation \u2014 but a foundation with no building = an empty lot. One real model per month = 12 valuations a year = a portfolio no certificate can match.</div>`}
],
quiz: [
{q: "The strongest proof you're ready as an analyst?", opts: ["Explain every number in your model WITHOUT notes \u2014 including assumptions and boundaries", "A 100% mock score", "Memorizing every formula", "Having 2 capstones"], answer: 0,
 hints: ["Interviewers don't ask your score.", "What's tested: understanding, not memorization."],
 explain: "Score = the past; explanation without notes = living understanding. Analysts are judged on defending their numbers in front of doubters \u2014 exactly like Day 27."},
{q: "After graduating, the best drill to keep modeling skill?", opts: ["Re-read every lesson weekly", "1 real-issuer DCF model per month", "Re-memorize the cheatsheet daily", "No need \u2014 already graduated"], answer: 1,
 hints: ["Skill = muscle.", "Which trains judgment, not memory?"],
 explain: "One real issuer per month = 12 valuations a year on messy real-world data. Re-reading lessons = repeating theory; real modeling = training judgment \u2014 and judgment is what gets paid."},
{q: "'Walk me through your DCF' \u2014 the 5-minute answer structure?", opts: ["Read every model row", "Tell the company history for 5 minutes", "Just state the numbers", "Assumptions \u2192 FCFF \u2192 WACC \u2192 TV \u2192 sensitivity & conclusion"], answer: 3,
 hints: ["The interviewer = the Day-27 audience.", "The 10-minute structure in 5 minutes."],
 explain: "Assumptions \u2192 FCFF \u2192 WACC \u2192 TV \u2192 sensitivity: the same logic chain as the Day-27 presentation, compressed. Interviewers hear how you think, not numbers \u2014 the numbers are in the model."},
{q: "Which capstone belongs in your portfolio?", opts: ["Both \u2014 more is better", "The one whose every number you can defend in front of an interviewer", "The one with the best grade", "The thickest one"], answer: 1,
 hints: ["A portfolio = an invitation to be attacked.", "Remember the Day-27 rubric."],
 explain: "A portfolio = inviting people to attack your model. One fully defensible capstone beats two that wobble at the third question. Defense quality > file quantity."},
{q: "One sprint habit most worth keeping for your whole career?", opts: ["All-nighters before deadlines", "Working as fast as possible", "Written assumptions + formula audit before sending \u2014 no number without basis and without trail", "Memorizing every Excel shortcut"], answer: 2,
 hints: ["Which saved you on Days 19\u201321?", "A habit = what you do unprompted."],
 explain: "Written assumptions = your numbers can be tested; formula audit = your model can be trusted. These two habits separate analysts who are 'probably right' from those actually right \u2014 in this sprint and 10 years on."}
],
hell: [
{q: "A year from now, an interviewer asks: 'does your INDF thesis still hold?' Best answer?", opts: ["'The thesis was built on 2026 info; what still holds = the method. The data needs refreshing \u2014 and here are the triggers that would change my conclusion\u2026'", "'Of course \u2014 my model was right'", "'Forgot already'", "'No longer relevant'"], answer: 0,
 explain: "Theses expire; methods don't. The professional answer: acknowledge the time context, show the change triggers, offer an update. Defending a stale thesis = proof you stopped thinking after graduating."},
{q: "Your two capstones: INDF (consumer) and a bank. Interviewer: 'pick ONE for 20 minutes.' Selection criteria?", opts: ["The one with the best numbers", "The one you're most ready to defend \u2014 every assumption grounded and you know its boundaries", "The newest one", "The thickest one"], answer: 1,
 explain: "20 minutes = 20 minutes under attack. Pick the ground you master best, not the most impressive-looking paper. Honesty about a model's limits = plus points, not minus."},
{q: "When must you say NO to an investment thesis \u2014 even your own?", opts: ["Never \u2014 a thesis must be defended to the death", "When the price drops 5%", "When one key assumption collapses on new data and no honest replacement assumption exists", "When others disagree"], answer: 2,
 explain: "Day 27 taught 'when to change your mind' as specific triggers. A thesis = a hypothesis, not an identity. The best analysts aren't never wrong \u2014 they're fastest to admit it."},
{q: "Your friend wants to join this sprint. One honest sentence from you about these 30 days?", opts: ["'Easy \u2014 relax, you'll pass'", "'Don't join \u2014 too hard'", "'Just join, the certificate is what matters'", "'30 days won't make you an expert \u2014 but they'll make you think like an analyst: written assumptions, tested numbers, a defensible thesis'"], answer: 3,
 explain: "Closing honesty: this sprint is a foundation, not a crown. What you take home isn't 'can do DCF' \u2014 it's a way of thinking: write your assumptions, test your numbers, defend your thesis. That stays useful long after every formula is forgotten."}
],
tasks: [
{t: "Task A \u2014 Verify the graduation checklist", d: "Check the 8 items above one by one \u2014 with evidence, not feelings. Anything empty = back to its day, today."},
{t: "Task B \u2014 Write a 1-page reflection", d: "3 things: (1) your weakest area on Day 1 vs now, (2) 1 thesis you dare defend, (3) your 90-day target."},
{t: "Task C \u2014 Celebrate \ud83c\udf93", d: "30 days, 3 gates, 3 mocks, 2 capstones. Mark your graduation your way \u2014 then start the roadmap: 1 issuer next month."}
]
};
